TASK-322 runtime harness: the Quick Detour bar's back button, the address
capsule's empty space opening the palette, and the capsule's load progress.
Temporary code, never committed to Detour/. Gated on DETOUR_VERIFY_DIR;
DETOUR_VERIFY_APPEARANCE light or dark; DETOUR_VERIFY_FIXTURE a loopback
server whose / is fast and whose /slow.html loads a script that trickles in
over ~10 s (a 30-line python3 ThreadingHTTPServer on 8937). Data dir
DetourVerify3220. Real HID moves and clicks (CGEvent .cghidEventTap); the
run script screencaptures -l on "SHOOT <name> <window>" log lines and kills
only the PID it launched after "done". The harness's own HID move counts as
input to its 1 s idle guard: leave a pause after a hover before the next
click, or that click is skipped (ABORT in the log).

    git apply docs/harness/task322-harness.patch && xcodegen generate
    xcodebuild -scheme Detour -configuration Debug build -derivedDataPath .derived
    run.sh light; run.sh dark
    git apply -R docs/harness/task322-harness.patch && xcodegen generate

Results (2026-11-13, light and dark): back button at x 76-100, clear of the
zoom button (ends at 72), the capsule from 106; disabled on the first page
(dimmed: a tinted template image draws the same disabled), enabled after
loading /slow.html, and a HID click on it went back (disabled again). Mid
load the capsule filled to WebKit's estimate (0.5, 299 of 597 pt) and
cleared (alpha 0) when the load ended. Hovering the capsule lightened it; a
HID click on the empty space between the pill and the host, and one just
left of the host (the lock), opened the palette prefilled with the URL. The
first dark run found a HID click on Copy Link opening the palette: under
the pointer a HoverButton shows its hover background, the hit landed on
that plain subview and went to the host; fixed (the capsule keeps any hit
inside a button), after which Copy Link copied and left the palette shut.
Not checked: a drag from the capsule's empty space by hand (the host's drag
path, covered by the unit tests), VoiceOver, several displays.

